--TEST--
`secret == secret` is a constant-time comparison that answers exactly what the ordinary one does — ADR 0033 §§ 1, 5 and ADR 0090 § 1
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0033 § 5: a comparison whose operands are `secret` lowers to a
// constant-time helper rather than the short-circuiting row every other
// `string` pair takes. The point of this case is that the *answer* is
// unchanged — the section buys a timing property, not a semantics — so every
// row below is one an unqualified pair would answer the same way. That the
// rest of a qualified value's answers are unmoved too -- concatenation,
// interpolation, the `bytes` round trip -- is the neighbouring
// a-tainted-value-answers-what-its-unqualified-twin-does.nvst.
secret string $expected = "s3cr3t-token";
secret string $correct = "s3cr3t-token";
secret string $wrong = "s3cr3t-tokeN";
secret string $shorter = "s3cr3t-toke";
secret string $empty = "";

if ($expected == $correct) { echo "equal\n"; } else { echo "NOT equal\n"; }
// Differs in the last byte alone: the row a short-circuiting comparison
// answers slowest, and the one § 5 exists for.
if ($expected == $wrong) { echo "BAD\n"; } else { echo "last byte differs\n"; }
// A length mismatch is `false` at once. § 5 does not hide lengths, and
// `Core\Hash::equals` says why: a token's length is not the secret.
if ($expected == $shorter) { echo "BAD\n"; } else { echo "length differs\n"; }
if ($empty == $empty) { echo "empty equals itself\n"; } else { echo "BAD\n"; }
// `!=` is the same helper negated, not a second row that could short-circuit.
if ($expected != $wrong) { echo "not equal\n"; } else { echo "BAD\n"; }
if ($expected != $correct) { echo "BAD\n"; } else { echo "not unequal\n"; }

// ADR 0033 § 1 puts the qualifier on `bytes` too, and `as bytes` is total and
// free (ADR 0009 § 3), so the same rows hold one base over.
secret bytes $mac = "abc" as bytes;
secret bytes $same = "abc" as bytes;
secret bytes $other = "abd" as bytes;
if ($mac == $same) { echo "bytes equal\n"; } else { echo "BAD\n"; }
if ($mac == $other) { echo "BAD\n"; } else { echo "bytes differ\n"; }
--EXPECT--
equal
last byte differs
length differs
empty equals itself
not equal
not unequal
bytes equal
bytes differ
